@ 37.2.1 - Proto-Siouan to Catawba
# citation: Pogostick Man, from Wolff, Hans (1950), “Comparative Siouan I”. International Journal of American Linguistics 16(2):61 – 66; Wolff, Hans (1950), “Comparative Siouan II” International Journal of American Linguistics 16(3):113 – 121; Wolff, Hans (1950), “Comparative Siouan III”. International Journal of American Linguistics 16(4):168 – 178; and cedh audmanh’s changes above, which assisted me in deciphering the vintage phonetic transcription scheme NB: Does not include developments in unstressed non-nasal vowels. L was apparently either /ɹ/ or /l/; Lʲ (Lʸ in the text) was apparently /j/, or maybe /ʎ/. Also, the changes of /p/ before a consonant are unclear, as described within the text. Changes appended with an asterisk are putative; there was a seeming lack of material for this language, so I’ve attempted to do some tracking work from the examples given in the text.
	{ ç, x } > { x, ʃ }
	p > p, b, m, w / _C
	p > b / V_V (*)
	tʲ > ʃ / in
	tʲ > ʒ
	t > ∅ / _k, when medial
	”V:[+nas] > Vn (*)
	∅ > ʔ / C_# (only